window.docContents[25].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___7___-Examples-of-algebras/Representations-and-ideals-of-the-Weyl-algebra/#Chapter2___Problem2___7___4":{"contents":"Problem 2.7.4. Let A be the Weyl algebra.\n\n(a) If \\operatorname{char} k = 0, what are the finite dimensional representations of A? What are the two-sided ideals in A?\n\nHint: For the first question, use the fact that for two square matrices B, C, \\operatorname{Tr}(BC) = \\operatorname{Tr}(CB). For the second question, show that any nonzero two-sided ideal in A contains a nonzero polynomial in x, and use this to characterize this ideal.\n\nSuppose for the rest of the problem that \\operatorname{char} k = p.\n(b) What is the center of A?\n\nHint: Show that x^p and y^p are central elements.\n\n(c) Find all irreducible finite dimensional representations of A.\n\nHint: Let V be an irreducible finite dimensional representation of A, and let v be an eigenvector of y in V. Show that the collection of vectors \\{v, xv, x^2 v, \\ldots, x^{p-1} v\\} is a basis of V.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.7. Examples of algebras\u0009Representations and ideals of the Weyl algebra","header":"Representations and ideals of the Weyl algebra","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___7___-Examples-of-algebras/Representations-and-ideals-of-the-Weyl-algebra/#Chapter2___Problem2___7___4"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___8___-Quivers/Representations-of-quivers-as-representations-of-path-algebras/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___8___-Quivers--Representations-of-quivers-as-representations-of-path-algebras--Representations-of-quivers-as-representations-of-path-algebras--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"An auxiliary type associated with a field and a quiver.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Definition2.8.4; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Discussion_path_algebra_intro; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.8. Quivers\u0009Representations of quivers as representations of path algebras\u0009Representations of quivers as representations of path algebras\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___8___-Quivers/Representations-of-quivers-as-representations-of-path-algebras/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___8___-Quivers--Representations-of-quivers-as-representations-of-path-algebras--Representations-of-quivers-as-representations-of-path-algebras--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___10___-Frobenius-determinant/Determinant-of-a-generic-matrix-is-irreducible/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results--4___10___-Frobenius-determinant--Determinant-of-a-generic-matrix-is-irreducible--Determinant-of-a-generic-matrix-is-irreducible--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"For every positive natural index, the determinant of the associated auxiliary matrix is irreducible.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter4/Lemma4.10.3; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.10. Frobenius determinant\u0009Determinant of a generic matrix is irreducible\u0009Determinant of a generic matrix is irreducible\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___10___-Frobenius-determinant/Determinant-of-a-generic-matrix-is-irreducible/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results--4___10___-Frobenius-determinant--Determinant-of-a-generic-matrix-is-irreducible--Determinant-of-a-generic-matrix-is-irreducible--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___3___-Examples/Irreducible-representations-of-S_4/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results--4___3___-Examples--Irreducible-representations-of-S_4--Irreducible-representations-of-S_4--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"The permutation group on four elements has five conjugacy classes.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter4/Example4.3_S4; role=supporting]\n\nThe monoid homomorphism describing the induced permutation action on three indices.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter4/Example4.3_S4; role=supporting]\n\nA finite-dimensional representation whose character is the induced fixed-point count minus one.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter4/Example4.3_S4; role=supporting]\n\nA finite-dimensional representation whose character is the four-coordinate fixed-point count minus one.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter4/Example4.3_S4; role=supporting]\n\nThe displayed squares of one, one, two, three, and three sum to the cardinality of the permutation group on four elements.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter4/Example4.3_S4; role=supporting]\n\nThe representation obtained by scaling each action operator by a unit-valued character.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter4/Example4.3_S4; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.3. Examples\u0009Irreducible representations of S_4\u0009Irreducible representations of S_4\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___3___-Examples/Irreducible-representations-of-S_4/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results--4___3___-Examples--Irreducible-representations-of-S_4--Irreducible-representations-of-S_4--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___5___-Orthogonality-of-characters/Column-orthogonality-of-characters/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results--4___5___-Orthogonality-of-characters--Column-orthogonality-of-characters":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.5. Orthogonality of characters","header":"Column orthogonality of characters","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___5___-Orthogonality-of-characters/Column-orthogonality-of-characters/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results--4___5___-Orthogonality-of-characters--Column-orthogonality-of-characters"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___21___-Schur-polynomials/Definition-of-D_lambda-and-Schur-polynomials-S_lambda/#Chapter5___Discussion_Schur_polynomials":{"contents":"Let \\lambda = (\\lambda_1, \\ldots, \\lambda_p) be a partition of n, and let N \\geq p. Let\n\nD_\\lambda(x) = \\sum_{s \\in S_N} (-1)^s \\prod_{j=1}^{N} x_{s(j)}^{\\lambda_j + N - j} = \\det(x_i^{\\lambda_j + N - j}).\n\nDefine the polynomials\n\nS_\\lambda(x) := \\frac{D_\\lambda(x)}{D_0(x)}\n\n(clearly D_0(x) is just \\Delta(x)). It is easy to see that these are indeed polynomials, as D_\\lambda is antisymmetric and therefore must be divisible by \\Delta. The polynomials S_\\lambda are called the Schur polynomials.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.21. Schur polynomials\u0009Definition of D_lambda and Schur polynomials S_lambda","header":"Definition of D_lambda and Schur polynomials S_lambda","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___21___-Schur-polynomials/Definition-of-D_lambda-and-Schur-polynomials-S_lambda/#Chapter5___Discussion_Schur_polynomials"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___1___-The-definition-of-a-category/Sets-vs-classes_SEMI_-locally-small-categories/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___1___-The-definition-of-a-category--Sets-vs-classes_SEMI_-locally-small-categories":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.1. The definition of a category","header":"Sets vs classes; locally small categories","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___1___-The-definition-of-a-category/Sets-vs-classes_SEMI_-locally-small-categories/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___1___-The-definition-of-a-category--Sets-vs-classes_SEMI_-locally-small-categories"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___4___-Equivalence-of-categories/Examples-of-equivalent-categories/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___4___-Equivalence-of-categories--Examples-of-equivalent-categories--Examples-of-equivalent-categories--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"An equivalence between the left and right types equipped with their category structures.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter7/Discussion_after_Definition7.4.1; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.4. Equivalence of categories\u0009Examples of equivalent categories\u0009Examples of equivalent categories\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___4___-Equivalence-of-categories/Examples-of-equivalent-categories/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___4___-Equivalence-of-categories--Examples-of-equivalent-categories--Examples-of-equivalent-categories--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___6___-Adjoint-functors/Ubiquity-of-adjoint-functors/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___6___-Adjoint-functors--Ubiquity-of-adjoint-functors":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.6. Adjoint functors","header":"Ubiquity of adjoint functors","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___6___-Adjoint-functors/Ubiquity-of-adjoint-functors/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___6___-Adjoint-functors--Ubiquity-of-adjoint-functors"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___5___-Blocks/Blocks-of-semisimple___-local___-and-specific-algebras/#Chapter9___Example9___5___2___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.5. Blocks\u0009Blocks of semisimple, local, and specific algebras\u0009Blocks of semisimple, local, and specific algebras","header":"Formalization","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___5___-Blocks/Blocks-of-semisimple___-local___-and-specific-algebras/#Chapter9___Example9___5___2___formalization"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___6___-Finite-abelian-categories/Characterization-of-projective-generators-in-finite-abelian-categories/#Chapter9___Exercise9___6___3":{"contents":"Exercise 9.6.3. Show that in a finite abelian category, P is a projective generator if and only if for every simple object L, one has \\operatorname{Hom}(P, L) \\neq 0. Deduce that any finite abelian category has a projective generator.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.6. Finite abelian categories\u0009Characterization of projective generators in finite abelian categories","header":"Characterization of projective generators in finite abelian categories","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___6___-Finite-abelian-categories/Characterization-of-projective-generators-in-finite-abelian-categories/#Chapter9___Exercise9___6___3"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___6___-Finite-abelian-categories/Finite-abelian-categories-are-module-categories/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___6___-Finite-abelian-categories--Finite-abelian-categories-are-module-categories--Finite-abelian-categories-are-module-categories--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"Under the displayed field-linear hypotheses, the opposite endomorphism ring is noetherian.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Theorem9.6.4; role=supporting]\n\nA functor from the given category to finitely generated modules over the opposite endomorphism\nring of the given object.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Theorem9.6.4; role=supporting]\n\nWith a noetherian opposite endomorphism ring, the associated module functor is essentially\nsurjective.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Theorem9.6.4; role=supporting]\n\nThe given object satisfies the additional displayed property.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Theorem9.6.4; role=supporting]\n\nMorphisms out of the given object form a finite module over its opposite endomorphism ring.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Theorem9.6.4; role=supporting]\n\nThe given object is a separator.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Theorem9.6.4; role=supporting]\n\nThe preadditive coyoneda functor at the given object is faithful.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Theorem9.6.4; role=supporting]\n\nThe preadditive coyoneda functor at the given object is full.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Theorem9.6.4; role=supporting]\n\nThe displayed categorical assumption entails finite biproducts.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Theorem9.6.4; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.6. Finite abelian categories\u0009Finite abelian categories are module categories\u0009Finite abelian categories are module categories\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___6___-Finite-abelian-categories/Finite-abelian-categories-are-module-categories/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___6___-Finite-abelian-categories--Finite-abelian-categories-are-module-categories--Finite-abelian-categories-are-module-categories--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___7___-Morita-equivalence/Basic-algebra/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___7___-Morita-equivalence--Basic-algebra--Basic-algebra--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"A property of a ring equipped with an algebra structure over a field.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Definition9.7.2; role=primary]\n\nA proposition associated with a ring algebra over a field.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Definition9.7.2; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.7. Morita equivalence\u0009Basic algebra\u0009Basic algebra\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___7___-Morita-equivalence/Basic-algebra/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___7___-Morita-equivalence--Basic-algebra--Basic-algebra--Formalization--Primary-declarations"}});